              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The typical jersey numbers for a linebacker (LB) in American football depend heavily on the era, the level of play (NFL, college, or high school), and the specific type of linebacker (inside vs. outside/edge rusher). \n\nIn the modern era, linebacker numbers are more diverse than ever, but historically, they have been highly standardized.\n\n---\n\n### 1. NFL Jersey Number Rules\nIn the NFL, jersey numbers are regulated by official league rules. Those rules have expanded significantly in recent years.\n\n* **The Modern Rule (2023\u2013Present):** Linebackers are allowed to wear **0\u201359 and 90\u201399**.\n* **The Traditional Rules (Pre-2021):** For decades, linebackers were strictly limited. From 1973 to 1984, they could only wear **50\u201359**. In 1984, the **90\u201399** range was added to accommodate 3-4 defenses, and in 2015, the **40\u201349** range was opened up. \n\n#### **The Traditional 50s (50\u201359)**\nThis is the most iconic and classic range for linebackers, particularly **inside and middle linebackers (MLBs)** who act as the \"quarterbacks of the defense.\" \n* **Classic Examples:** \n  * **51:** Dick Butkus\n  * **52:** Ray Lewis, Patrick Willis\n  * **54:** Brian Urlacher, Fred Warner, Bobby Wagner\n  * **55:** Junior Seau, Derrick Brooks\n  * **56:** Lawrence Taylor\n\n#### **The Edge-Rusher 90s (90\u201399)**\nThis range is highly typical for **outside linebackers (OLBs)** who specialize in rushing the passer (often referred to as \"Edge\" defenders).\n* **Classic Examples:**\n  * **90:** T.J. Watt\n  * **97:** Joey Bosa (often plays a hybrid DE/OLB role)\n  * **99:** Jason Taylor\n\n#### **The Modern Single-Digits & Teens (0\u201319)**\nSince the NFL relaxed its numbering rules in 2021 (and added the number 0 in 2023), many younger, faster linebackers have opted for single-digit numbers or \"skill position\" numbers that they wore in college. \n* **Modern Examples:**\n  * **0:** Roquan Smith\n  * **4:** Nick Herbig\n  * **6:** Patrick Queen\n  * **11:** Micah Parsons\n\n---\n\n### 2. College Football (NCAA)\nIn college football, numbering rules are incredibly relaxed. Linebackers can wear **any number from 0 to 99** (the only restriction is that two players on the same team cannot be on the field at the same time wearing the same number). \n\n* In college, it is incredibly common for star linebackers to wear **single digits** (like 1, 4, 5, or 11) because they are viewed as prestigious \"playmaker\" numbers.\n\n---\n\n### 3. High School Football (NFHS)\nUnder National Federation of State High School Associations (NFHS) rules, there are no strict positional numbering requirements for defensive players. \n* High school linebackers can wear **any number (0\u201349 and 80\u201399)**. \n* *(Note: High school offensive linemen must wear 50\u201379, which is why linebackers usually avoid those numbers in high school, though they can technically wear them if they do not play offense).*\n* Most high school linebackers opt for numbers in the **40s, 10s, 20s, or single digits** to mimic their favorite college and NFL players.",
